<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sæberht of Essex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 616</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Saeberht of Essex</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sæberht was king of the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> from about 604 until about 616 and the first Christian king of Essex. A nephew of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelberht-of-kent">Æthelberht of Kent</a>, he accepted baptism and allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellitus">Mellitus</a> to establish a bishopric in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, but his sons returned to paganism after his death. A rich burial found at Prittlewell in 2003 may be his.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saeberht-of-essex.jpg" alt="Sæberht of Essex" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Speed · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sebert_-_John_Speed.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The genealogies and regnal lists agree that Sæberht was the son of King Sledd, who may have been regarded as the founder of the East Saxon dynasty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> names his mother as Ricula, a sister of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelberht-of-kent">Æthelberht of Kent</a>. The main source for his reign is Bede's Historia Ecclesiastica gentis Anglorum of the early 8th century, whose account of the mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellitus">Mellitus</a> came from Abbot Albinus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> priest Nothhelm; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> and later genealogies add a little.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 604 Augustine consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellitus">Mellitus</a> bishop in the province of the East Saxons, whose capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, making him the first Saxon Bishop of London. Bede tells that Sæberht was converted and baptised by Mellitus, while his three sons stayed pagan. The episcopal church built in London was probably founded by Æthelberht rather than Sæberht, and a charter claiming to record a grant of lands from Æthelberht to Mellitus is a forgery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Æthelberht and Sæberht both died in 616, leaving the Gregorian mission without strong patrons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says that Sæberht's sons, two of whom a genealogy names as Sexred and Sæward, drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellitus">Mellitus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> after he refused them a taste of the sacramental bread.</p><p>Later medieval legend made Sæberht and his wife Ethelgoda the founders of a monastery of St Peter on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>. During Henry III's rebuilding their supposed remains were moved to a tomb in the Chapter House, and in 1308 reportedly to a marble tomb in the south ambulatory, though modern scholars doubt the bones were ever his. A local tradition places his home and burial at Great Burstead in Essex.</p><p>The Prittlewell burial found in 2003, whose gold foil crosses mark an early Christian, was at first thought most likely to be his. Carbon dating later placed the tomb between 575 and 605, and in 2019 specialists of the Museum of London Archaeology suggested it could be that of Seaxa, Sæberht's brother.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sæberht of Essex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their royal genealogy traces kings to Aescwine, traditionally placed in 527, and later kings bore names beginning with S, such as <b>Saeberht</b>, Sigeberht and Swithred.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-essex">Kingdom of Essex</a></li></ul></section>
